#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157135 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157429 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "04309d45-2991-5d4b-9dc9-b75422610ab6" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inequality When the Base of the Exponential is Greater Than One"}] :question/id #uuid "e5b64c26-300d-530d-b22f-ff061f4e3296" :question/math-academy-id "e-7657" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $4e^x - 5 >  0.$" :question/worked-solution "Isolating the exponential term, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{e}^{x}-5 & >0 \\\\ 4{e}^{x} & >5 \\\\ {e}^{x} & >\\frac{5}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, taking the natural logarithm of both sides and applying the laws of logarithms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{x}) & >\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{5}{4}) \\\\ x & >\\operatorname{ln}⁡5-\\operatorname{ln}⁡4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157136 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157432 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "29435c7e-2913-51cb-b1ca-bb88c5109d0a" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inequality with Exponential Functions on Both Sides"}] :question/id #uuid "95104c90-f452-5551-adec-836ef1192276" :question/math-academy-id "e-7660" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $3 \\cdot 5^x <   7^{3-x}.$" :question/worked-solution "Taking the natural logarithm of both sides and applying the laws of logarithms, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\cdot {5}^{x} & <{7}^{3-x} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(3\\cdot {5}^{x}) & <\\operatorname{ln}⁡({7}^{3-x}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡3+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({5}^{x}) & <\\operatorname{ln}⁡({7}^{3-x}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡3+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡5 & <(3-x)\\operatorname{ln}⁡7 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡3+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡5 & <3\\operatorname{ln}⁡7-x\\operatorname{ln}⁡7 \\\\ x\\operatorname{ln}⁡5+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡7 & <3\\operatorname{ln}⁡7-\\operatorname{ln}⁡3 \\\\ x(\\operatorname{ln}⁡5+\\operatorname{ln}⁡7) & <3\\operatorname{ln}⁡7-\\operatorname{ln}⁡3 \\\\ x & <\\frac{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡7-\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5+\\operatorname{ln}⁡7}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157142 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157430 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "05f94d64-fdf7-55d8-80fb-3988a47ed2d4" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inequality When the Base of the Exponential is Between Zero and One"}] :question/id #uuid "1c336514-aac7-5f9c-8118-8a16188f2d42" :question/math-academy-id "e-7703" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $5 \\cdot \\left( \\dfrac{3}{4} \\right)^x + 2 \\geq 12.$" :question/worked-solution "Isolating the exponential term, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5\\cdot {(\\frac{3}{4})}^{x}+2 & \\ge 12 \\\\ 5\\cdot {(\\frac{3}{4})}^{x} & \\ge 10 \\\\ {(\\frac{3}{4})}^{x} & \\ge 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, taking the natural logarithm of both sides and applying the laws of logarithms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡[{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{x}] & \\ge \\operatorname{ln}⁡2 \\\\ x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{3}{4}) & \\ge \\operatorname{ln}⁡2 \\\\ x(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-\\operatorname{ln}⁡4) & \\ge \\operatorname{ln}⁡2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, note that $\\ln 3 - \\ln 4$ is a negative quantity, so when we divide both sides by it, we need to flip the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & \\le \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157143 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157431 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a114450f-bfcd-5db3-9060-fcd067c606fa" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inequality When the Exponential is Compared to Zero or a Negative Number"}] :question/id #uuid "185e4c00-f218-5d54-8345-397267a74b6f" :question/math-academy-id "e-7704" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $4 \\cdot 3^{8-x} + 12 > 0.$" :question/worked-solution "Isolating the exponential term, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\cdot {3}^{8-x}+12 & >0 \\\\ 4\\cdot {3}^{8-x} & >-12 \\\\ {3}^{8-x} & >-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause a power of a positive number is always greater than $0,$ we must have\n\n$$\n3^{8-x} > 0\n$$\n\nfor all values of $x.$\n\nTherefore, the inequality $3^{8-x} > -3$ is satisfied for all real values of $x.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250359 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157430 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "05f94d64-fdf7-55d8-80fb-3988a47ed2d4" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inequality When the Base of the Exponential is Between Zero and One"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "018d8348-3190-5eea-b417-c63541e4aff5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247430} {:answer/id #uuid "08eab5fa-3385-5df5-b8fd-2f29432c498e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\le \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247499} {:answer/id #uuid "1eb0449b-6ed5-5ab7-9e17-f2f9074c0612" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\le \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247696} {:answer/id #uuid "2e1524c0-3b53-58d5-b338-ecb0ead5ac3f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247832} {:answer/id #uuid "67f62f68-93fb-55cb-a9ad-f01fff119dc4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248357}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "018d8348-3190-5eea-b417-c63541e4aff5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247430} :answer-field/id #uuid "078894b3-54fc-5c41-9c56-f018aef4c9b4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249683}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9725f40a-ccc5-5b84-9193-a09e8482b8d4" :question/math-academy-id "q-115218" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $\\frac{1}{3}{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x+1}\\le \\frac{1}{9}.$" :question/worked-solution "Isolating the exponential term, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x+1} & \\le \\frac{1}{9} \\\\ {(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x+1} & \\le \\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, taking the natural logarithm of both sides and applying the laws of logarithms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡[{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x+1}] & \\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\ (x+1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{2}) & \\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\ (x+1)(\\operatorname{ln}⁡1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) & \\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡3 \\\\ (x+1)(-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) & \\le -\\operatorname{ln}⁡3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, note that $-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2$ is a negative quantity, so when we divide both sides by it, we need to flip the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+1 & \\ge \\frac{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\ x & \\ge \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250364 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157431 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a114450f-bfcd-5db3-9060-fcd067c606fa" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inequality When the Exponential is Compared to Zero or a Negative Number"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "026efd52-bc0e-598f-8510-56c0f360eb76" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x>-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247441} {:answer/id #uuid "26cc08ff-87ed-524a-b566-888354bcfb6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x>2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247766} {:answer/id #uuid "60613cd8-1bd4-5bcc-9818-293168e6db7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248299} {:answer/id #uuid "abff0774-73f0-51c2-ada7-120345b1c0af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "all real numbers" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248955} {:answer/id #uuid "c06fecbc-699f-5e65-a769-42ba1444c6d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "no solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249136}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "abff0774-73f0-51c2-ada7-120345b1c0af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "all real numbers" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248955} :answer-field/id #uuid "1e1b1c42-32ff-57b4-83ab-04ed1baf84d6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249723}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ced4ffe4-e7bc-5404-a238-528823539017" :question/math-academy-id "q-115231" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $\\frac{3}{2}(0.43)^{4x+5}+4>-2.$" :question/worked-solution "Isolating the exponential term, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2}(0.43)^{4x+5}+4 & >-2 \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}(0.43)^{4x+5} & >-6 \\\\ (0.43)^{4x+5} & >-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause a power of a positive number is always greater than $0,$ we must have\n\n$$\n(0.43)^{4x+5}>0\n$$\n\nfor all values of $x.$\n\nTherefore, the inequality $(0.43)^{4x+5}>-4$ is satisfied for all real values of $x.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250368 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157432 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "29435c7e-2913-51cb-b1ca-bb88c5109d0a" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inequality with Exponential Functions on Both Sides"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "394737bc-773a-5052-8889-796adf884851" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x>\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5+\\operatorname{ln}⁡7}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5-\\operatorname{ln}⁡7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247932} {:answer/id #uuid "3ed1b089-fa72-501c-a9f2-f3ec109cf231" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x>\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡7}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡7+\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247979} {:answer/id #uuid "a77efff0-96b8-5338-a4e0-5761d1e89e51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x>-\\frac{7}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248912} {:answer/id #uuid "cf85cd4c-7629-5a3b-801d-506323092847" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x>\\frac{5}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249266} {:answer/id #uuid "dad2bd11-14ed-5f74-ac33-0ded3a42bf6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x>\\operatorname{ln}⁡7-\\operatorname{ln}⁡5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249357}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "394737bc-773a-5052-8889-796adf884851" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x>\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5+\\operatorname{ln}⁡7}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5-\\operatorname{ln}⁡7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247932} :answer-field/id #uuid "d44037dc-fa0e-5905-a29e-40cda249ba9b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250038}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7dea1588-01f3-5e0d-9811-784ff06fa34a" :question/math-academy-id "q-115248" :question/problem "Solve the inequality ${7}^{x+1}>{5}^{x-1}.$" :question/worked-solution "Taking the natural logarithm of both sides and applying the laws of logarithms, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{7}^{x+1} & >{5}^{x-1} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({7}^{x+1}) & >\\operatorname{ln}⁡({5}^{x-1}) \\\\ (x+1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡7 & >(x-1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡5 \\\\ x\\operatorname{ln}⁡7+\\operatorname{ln}⁡7 & >x\\operatorname{ln}⁡5-\\operatorname{ln}⁡5 \\\\ x\\operatorname{ln}⁡7-x\\operatorname{ln}⁡5 & >-\\operatorname{ln}⁡5-\\operatorname{ln}⁡7 \\\\ x(\\operatorname{ln}⁡7-\\operatorname{ln}⁡5) & >-\\operatorname{ln}⁡5-\\operatorname{ln}⁡7 \\\\ x & >-\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5+\\operatorname{ln}⁡7}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡7-\\operatorname{ln}⁡5} \\\\ x & >\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5+\\operatorname{ln}⁡7}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5-\\operatorname{ln}⁡7}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157736 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157429 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "04309d45-2991-5d4b-9dc9-b75422610ab6" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inequality When the Base of the Exponential is Greater Than One"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/id #uuid "2317ac7a-6810-5ebd-b759-0ad3059d4d54" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155258} {:answer-field/id #uuid "32f00e3e-0ee2-5b85-a1c5-2b6e6779f01e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155259}] :question/id #uuid "4a7e522c-a79d-5bda-94b9-530843463aa8" :question/math-academy-id "q-276944" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $2 \\cdot 8^{2x} < \\frac{1}{3}$.$x$\n{{field-1}} {{field-2}}"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294926 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157429 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "04309d45-2991-5d4b-9dc9-b75422610ab6" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inequality When the Base of the Exponential is Greater Than One"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ea38e6be-f014-5512-a1ba-8ccb320a90bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{log}_{4}(14)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293846}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ea38e6be-f014-5512-a1ba-8ccb320a90bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{log}_{4}(14)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293846} :answer-field/id #uuid "12bd06f5-c128-5ece-a1ad-42f93aab1e1c" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293989} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d80fb632-82ac-5a2d-82ff-8e718314f84a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value ">" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293771}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d80fb632-82ac-5a2d-82ff-8e718314f84a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value ">" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293771} :answer-field/id #uuid "2a79b385-65a0-5ead-8571-f9e6b4e68eee" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294072}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6d5edd14-7e7b-53cf-8d2d-da6793a07e0a" :question/math-academy-id "q-277846" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {4}^{x}>7.$\n\n$x$\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "Isolating the exponential term, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {4}^{x} & >7 \\\\ {4}^{x} & >14.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, taking the logarithm to base $4$ of both sides and applying the laws of logarithms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{4}⁡({4}^{x}) & >{log}_{4}⁡(14) \\\\ x & >{log}_{4}⁡(14).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323811 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157432 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "29435c7e-2913-51cb-b1ca-bb88c5109d0a" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inequality with Exponential Functions on Both Sides"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e8b3b6f-beec-552d-9868-ed6a0f8961df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x<\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321259} {:answer/id #uuid "1c557786-2967-59f8-9bd5-79da655905ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x>\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321362} {:answer/id #uuid "5f13a860-aaa5-50a7-bd99-bf37fedc3d28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x>\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡3+\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321896} {:answer/id #uuid "7aeb5e32-2058-558c-8345-f421ebabdca7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x<\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡3+\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322111} {:answer/id #uuid "cd89b622-3090-56f6-b614-bf5d1b191223" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x>\\frac{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322748}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1c557786-2967-59f8-9bd5-79da655905ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x>\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321362} :answer-field/id #uuid "ec706b48-8cdc-560f-b7d1-8876a9a34e0e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323634}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4215e98b-bff5-5507-9fa2-a2d9f87a2aa6" :question/math-academy-id "q-365326" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $3{(\\frac{1}{5})}^{x-2}>{3}^{2-3x}.$" :question/worked-solution "Taking the natural logarithm of both sides and applying the laws of logarithms, we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{(\\frac{1}{5})}^{x-2} & >{3}^{2-3x} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡[3{(\\frac{1}{5})}^{x-2}] & >\\operatorname{ln}⁡({3}^{2-3x}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡3+\\operatorname{ln}⁡[{(\\frac{1}{5})}^{x-2}] & >\\operatorname{ln}⁡({3}^{2-3x}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡3+(x-2)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{5}) & >(2-3x)\\operatorname{ln}⁡3 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡3+(x-2)(\\operatorname{ln}⁡1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡5) & >(2-3x)\\operatorname{ln}⁡3 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡3+(x-2)(0-\\operatorname{ln}⁡5) & >(2-3x)\\operatorname{ln}⁡3 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡3+(2-x)\\operatorname{ln}⁡5 & >(2-3x)\\operatorname{ln}⁡3 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡3+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡5-x\\operatorname{ln}⁡5 & >2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-3x\\operatorname{ln}⁡3 \\\\ 3x\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-x\\operatorname{ln}⁡5 & >2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡5 \\\\ x(3\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-\\operatorname{ln}⁡5) & >\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡5 \\\\ x & >\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157896 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157429 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "04309d45-2991-5d4b-9dc9-b75422610ab6" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inequality When the Base of the Exponential is Greater Than One"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aa5f9405-a736-586d-8d70-1e26463d7fb0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x < \\ln 4 - \\ln 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156298} {:answer/id #uuid "d50f3159-cb6c-51bc-bd30-4d9337968ac1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x > \\ln 4 - \\ln 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156299} {:answer/id #uuid "eb6b26e6-e437-5365-bb55-f98a8d2f1d44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x > \\ln 3 - \\ln 4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156300} {:answer/id #uuid "ebbefecd-a5f5-516f-9ed5-9369e9485416" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x < \\ln 3 - \\ln 4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156301} {:answer/id #uuid "2efd6b4c-17c0-50fc-bb15-72687e0c2b9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x < \\ln 3 + \\ln 4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156302}] :answer-field/id #uuid "83f053be-9090-5811-8315-530372e873fa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156297}] :question/id #uuid "1b7ad171-3d97-5b1b-b8d8-95af987a1b58" :question/math-academy-id "q-87581" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $3e^{x} - 4 < 0$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157899 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157431 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a114450f-bfcd-5db3-9060-fcd067c606fa" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inequality When the Exponential is Compared to Zero or a Negative Number"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "817707d6-ed5e-5281-9206-d4068e5f8214" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x > \\log_{5} (2) - 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156316} {:answer/id #uuid "96b61ea8-195c-57d8-8a35-b7bdb9f2ff8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "all real numbers" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156317} {:answer/id #uuid "b0505df1-3d3f-5cb6-897f-7d5ba616d0e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x > 3 - \\log_{5} (2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156318} {:answer/id #uuid "b5ded0b6-547f-510a-8787-f44bf3ea2fc3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "no solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156319} {:answer/id #uuid "404ca675-c517-5ffb-9803-6932ecd44af5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x < 3 - \\log_{5} (2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156320}] :answer-field/id #uuid "593effad-5f88-50e4-96ba-9db1dece841d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156315}] :question/id #uuid "55f2f971-d19d-5674-bd56-577acd359679" :question/math-academy-id "q-87594" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $2 \\cdot 5^{3 - x} + 4 > 0$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354597 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157430 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "05f94d64-fdf7-55d8-80fb-3988a47ed2d4" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inequality When the Base of the Exponential is Between Zero and One"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21c40639-3c77-50c8-8f1c-bbadeb578a95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x>\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\operatorname{ln}⁡9}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351873} {:answer/id #uuid "762d8584-356c-5934-bef4-eaafc9ebfb22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x>\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\operatorname{ln}⁡9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352605} {:answer/id #uuid "b7b9eb79-14b7-5185-8a50-0fd215992e70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x>\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4+\\operatorname{ln}⁡9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353140} {:answer/id #uuid "db1e0f4d-4196-507d-b1ad-1cb812c5526a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x<\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\operatorname{ln}⁡9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353471} {:answer/id #uuid "fcda1a1b-27c7-5555-bcad-2e5b620275f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x<\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4+\\operatorname{ln}⁡9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353780}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "762d8584-356c-5934-bef4-eaafc9ebfb22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x>\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\operatorname{ln}⁡9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352605} :answer-field/id #uuid "f0f1dab6-f8cf-5d41-b492-b3aa875e7c0c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354235}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "653d874d-a486-580e-a2d8-67e03055d454" :question/math-academy-id "q-87828" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $5\\cdot {(\\frac{4}{9})}^{x}<25.$" :question/worked-solution "Isolating the exponential term, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5\\cdot {(\\frac{4}{9})}^{x} & <25 \\\\ {(\\frac{4}{9})}^{x} & <5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, taking the natural logarithm of both sides and applying the laws of logarithms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡[{(\\frac{4}{9})}^{x}] & <\\operatorname{ln}⁡5 \\\\ x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{4}{9}) & <\\operatorname{ln}⁡5 \\\\ x(\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\operatorname{ln}⁡9) & <\\operatorname{ln}⁡5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, note that $\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\operatorname{ln}⁡9$ is a negative quantity, so when we divide both sides by it, we need to flip the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & >\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\operatorname{ln}⁡9}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354610 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157431 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a114450f-bfcd-5db3-9060-fcd067c606fa" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inequality When the Exponential is Compared to Zero or a Negative Number"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23732147-0ec5-530e-8b60-fef4f9aef0d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "no solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351886} {:answer/id #uuid "ae126722-fc12-5191-a374-4e58c15fb502" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x<\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353044} {:answer/id #uuid "b51c2b64-41d9-519a-b633-676091e8f9fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x>\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353113} {:answer/id #uuid "c113b2fd-2e34-50e6-9d87-4f24e3685f4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x<\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353234} {:answer/id #uuid "c75a274e-9aa1-53dd-bf1b-0ff0ced8d750" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "all real numbers" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353295}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "23732147-0ec5-530e-8b60-fef4f9aef0d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "no solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351886} :answer-field/id #uuid "e03eebba-6afb-5ac5-a43d-5f0738e73b85" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354200}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "48ad6fff-2c0d-5121-b124-5aea1ae6b3d8" :question/math-academy-id "q-87876" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $-4-{e}^{3x}>2.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we isolate the exponential term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4-{e}^{3x} & >2 \\\\ -{e}^{3x} & >6 \\\\ {e}^{3x} & <-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, recall that a positive number raised to any real power is always positive. Since $e>0,$ we have\n\n$$\n{e}^{3x}>0\n$$\n\nfor every real value of $x.$\n\nTherefore, ${e}^{3x}$ cannot be less than $-6.$ Hence, the inequality\n\n$$\n{e}^{3x}<-6\n$$\n\nhas no solutions."}]}
